Non-envenomated minor domestic dog nip resulting in a superficial skin break only
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Shallow epidermal abrasion, Proportionate mild pain, Intact distal neurovascular status

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-031
Reason for Visit: Acute animal bite requiring physical assessment to rule out deep soft tissue crush injury or significant vascular compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute domestic dog bite with skin breakage. Urgent clinical evaluation was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to properly irrigate the wound, assess the depth of the puncture for tendon involvement, and rule out deep compartmental damage, which carries a severe risk of rapidly progressing polymicrobial infection.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My neighbor's dog nipped my hand, and it broke the skin, but it's not bleeding much.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a shallow epidermal abrasion and a non-penetrating small puncture wound sustained approximately 1 hour ago. Reports mild localized pain proportionate to the applied physical trauma. Notes minimal serosanguinous oozing without active pulsatile bleeding. There is a clear history of a provoked domestic canine interaction. Complete absence of rapidly spreading cellulitis, purulence, or numbness.
Functional Status: Fully independent, minor guarding of the affected hand.
Social/Safety Concerns: Potential tension with the neighbor; rabies vaccination status of the canine confirmed as up-to-date.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/82 | HR: 86 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, cooperative, slight anxiety regarding the bite.
Physical Exam: Hand examination reveals a shallow epidermal abrasion and small puncture, with trace surrounding erythema strictly limited to the immediate wound edges. Diagnostic triad confirmed: shallow epidermal abrasion, proportionate mild pain, and intact distal neurovascular status. Normal distal capillary refill and arterial pulses are present. Intact tendon function and preserved full active range of motion across all digits. Preserved two-point discrimination distal to the injury site. No underlying bony crepitus.
Environmental Observations: Basic first-aid kit open on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient's tetanus booster is up to date (last given 3 years ago).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Non-envenomated minor domestic dog nip resulting in a superficial skin break only - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep Tissue Crush Injury - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal capillary refill, absence of significant swelling, and fully preserved range of motion against resistance.

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Immediate acute presentation without time for bacterial incubation, and absence of spreading diffuse erythema.

Tendon Rupture/Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact active flexion and extension of all fingers with preserved strength.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained an acute animal bite. Due to the high risk of Capnocytophaga or Pasteurella multocida infection in deep dog bites, delaying assessment until regular clinic hours posed an unacceptable risk of missing a deep puncture wound or joint capsule penetration. The prompt physical examination confidently confirmed the injury was purely superficial without neurovascular or tendinous compromise, allowing for safe, conservative wound decontamination and averting a costly emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Aggressive irrigation of the wound with 500 mL normal saline and povidone-iodine solution. Application of a sterile non-occlusive dressing. Prescription for prophylactic oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for 3-5 days strictly as a precaution due to the mechanism of injury.
Positive Behavioral Change: Cleanse the wound twice daily with plain soap and running water for exactly two minutes, using the visual cue of a clear, non-red wound border as a reward for maintaining strict hygiene.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for a mandatory wound check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a high fever, if the hand becomes increasingly swollen and throbs intensely, or if red streaks begin moving up your arm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a superficial animal bite; the risk of secondary bacterial infection remains high despite early cleaning.
Clinical Course: The acute stinging will rapidly resolve. The wound will form a scab over 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you lose the ability to bend your fingers, if the wound drains thick pus, or if the redness spreads rapidly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Dog mouths carry bacteria that can cause severe infections if trapped under the skin. Although the bite is shallow, we use a short course of antibiotics and aggressive washing to kill any bacteria before they multiply.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics exactly as prescribed. Do not cover the wound with a tight, airtight bandage. Wash the area under running tap water twice daily.

